//! image/reading_order.rye -- the whole scene's TOTAL READING ORDER: fold image/scene_graph.rye's per-pair directional flags into one
//! top-to-bottom, left-to-right page order a surface reads to lay objects out, narrate a scene, or number a form -- exact integers, NO
//! float and NO tolerance.
//!
//! Season G (Open Media Primitives) -- the scene-read family names a POINT's object (image/hit_test.rye), a RECTANGLE's or POLYGON's
//! objects (image/region_select.rye, image/lasso_select.rye), a selection's FACTS and EXTENT (image/selection_summary.rye,
//! image/selection_bounds.rye), a PAIR's spatial relation (image/object_relation.rye), and the whole scene's pairwise STRUCTURE
//! (image/scene_graph.rye). This rung reads the whole scene as a SEQUENCE: `reading_order` returns every object's dense label in the
//! order a reader meets them -- earliest top edge first, then leftmost, then the earliest label to seal the tie.
//!
//! The DECISIVE crux -- the box-is-a-prefilter lesson lifted one last step, from a PARTIAL relation to a TOTAL order. image/scene_graph.rye
//! holds the four STRICT directional flags for every pair (a wholly above / below / left of / right of b). Those flags are a PARTIAL
//! order: two objects whose boxes overlap on BOTH axes are strictly neither, so every flag reads FALSE and the pair is left UNORDERED.
//! A reading order is a TOTAL order -- it must seat every object in one sequence, deciding even the pairs the flags leave silent -- so
//! the strict flags can never be topologically sorted into it; a key-based total order (top edge, then left edge, then label) is what
//! seats them. Two facts on metal say it:
//!
//!   THE READING ORDER OUTRUNS THE STRICT FLAGS. An L and a small block tucked in its concavity have OVERLAPPING boxes, so all four of
//!   `object_relation`'s directional flags read FALSE -- the scene graph leaves the pair unordered. Yet the L's top edge sits above the
//!   block's, so `reads_before(L, block)` is TRUE: the total order names a first where every strict flag is silent.
//!
//!   THE READING ORDER REFINES, IT NEVER CONTRADICTS. Where the boxes ARE strictly separated -- the L wholly above a lower object, so
//!   `a_above_b` reads TRUE -- the reading order AGREES, seating the L first. The total order refines the partial one: it adds order
//!   where the flags were silent, and never reverses order the flags asserted.
//!
//! And the order EARNS ITS KEEP over the raster label order it is built beside. Labels are assigned in raster order -- by each
//! component's first-encountered pixel (its topmost row, then the leftmost pixel IN that row). Reading order sorts by the box's LEFT
//! edge, the leftmost pixel ANYWHERE. A flag-shaped object whose top row sits to the right while its body reaches further left is
//! labeled AFTER a small block yet reads BEFORE it -- so the reading order genuinely reorders the labels, it is no mere relabeling.
//!
//! Compose not braid. This rung OWNS only the total-order comparator, the permutation, and the rank -- no label re-derived, no box
//! re-measured, no flag recomputed. image/scene_graph.rye owns the pairwise flag matrix the order refines; image/scene_manifest.rye owns
//! the per-object boxes the comparator reads; image/label.rye owns the component map. A reading over a reading over a reading over a
//! reading.
//!
//! Everything is bounded before a byte: the object count rode in under image/scene_graph.rye's `scene_graph_max_objects` cap, so the
//! permutation is that long and the selection sort runs a bounded count-squared comparisons, each an exact-integer box compare. A
//! background or past-the-count label refuses `BadObjectLabel`; a reads-before of an object with itself refuses `BadObjectLabel`, having
//! read nothing. No network, no key, no funds -- the reading order a surface names the day it must lay out a page, narrate a scene in
//! order, or number a form top to bottom, on our own open geometry.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run image/reading_order.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;
const label = @import("label.rye");
const qoi = @import("qoi.rye");
const scene_manifest = @import("scene_manifest.rye");
const scene_graph = @import("scene_graph.rye");

pub const reading_order_version = "20260819.001046";

pub const ReadingOrderError = error{
    BadObjectLabel,
};

/// TRUE when the object described by `ea` reads BEFORE the one described by `eb` -- the total-order comparator, exact integers and no
/// tolerance. The primary key is the top edge (`min_y`, earliest first), the secondary the left edge (`min_x`, leftmost first), and the
/// final the dense `label` (earliest first), which is distinct for distinct objects and so seals the order into a STRICT TOTAL one: for
/// any two distinct objects exactly one of `entry_reads_before(ea, eb)` and `entry_reads_before(eb, ea)` is true.
pub fn entry_reads_before(ea: scene_manifest.ObjectEntry, eb: scene_manifest.ObjectEntry) bool {
    // invariant: both entries name real, dense objects.
    assert(ea.label >= 1);
    assert(eb.label >= 1);
    // invariant: the comparator seats two DISTINCT objects; a well-formed order never compares one with itself.
    assert(ea.label != eb.label);
    if (ea.min_y != eb.min_y) return ea.min_y < eb.min_y;
    if (ea.min_x != eb.min_x) return ea.min_x < eb.min_x;
    return ea.label < eb.label;
}

/// TRUE when object `a` reads before object `b` in the scene's reading order -- a single pairwise answer read from the two boxes,
/// refining the scene graph's strict flags. Refuses `BadObjectLabel` for a background (0) or past-the-count label on either side, and
/// for `a == b`, having read nothing.
pub fn reads_before(graph: scene_graph.SceneGraph, a: u32, b: u32) !bool {
    // invariant: the manifest holds exactly one row per component.
    assert(graph.manifest.objects.len == @as(usize, graph.components));
    if (a == 0 or a > graph.components) return error.BadObjectLabel;
    if (b == 0 or b > graph.components) return error.BadObjectLabel;
    if (a == b) return error.BadObjectLabel;
    const ea = try scene_manifest.object_of(graph.manifest, a);
    const eb = try scene_manifest.object_of(graph.manifest, b);
    return entry_reads_before(ea, eb);
}

/// The whole scene's READING ORDER -- every object's dense label seated in one top-to-bottom, left-to-right sequence. A newly allocated
/// slice `order` of length `components`, `order[0]` the first object read and `order[components-1]` the last, always a PERMUTATION of
/// `1..components`. A selection sort by the total-order comparator, bounded count-squared under the scene graph's object cap.
pub fn reading_order(allocator: std.mem.Allocator, graph: scene_graph.SceneGraph) ![]u32 {
    // invariant: the manifest holds exactly one row per component.
    assert(graph.manifest.objects.len == @as(usize, graph.components));
    const n: u32 = graph.components;
    // invariant: the object count rode in under the scene graph's cap, so the permutation and the sort are bounded before a byte.
    assert(n <= scene_graph.scene_graph_max_objects);

    const order = try allocator.alloc(u32, @as(usize, n));
    // seed the sequence in dense label order, then sort it into reading order.
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < n) : (i += 1) order[@as(usize, i)] = i + 1;

    var s: u32 = 0;
    while (s < n) : (s += 1) {
        var best: u32 = s;
        var t: u32 = s + 1;
        while (t < n) : (t += 1) {
            const e_t = try scene_manifest.object_of(graph.manifest, order[@as(usize, t)]);
            const e_best = try scene_manifest.object_of(graph.manifest, order[@as(usize, best)]);
            // strictly-before keeps the earliest-reading object at each seat -- deterministic, since the comparator is a total order.
            if (entry_reads_before(e_t, e_best)) best = t;
        }
        if (best != s) {
            const tmp = order[@as(usize, s)];
            order[@as(usize, s)] = order[@as(usize, best)];
            order[@as(usize, best)] = tmp;
        }
    }

    // invariant: the reading order seats exactly one place per object.
    assert(order.len == @as(usize, n));
    return order;
}

/// The 1-based POSITION of object `a` in the reading order -- how many objects read before it, plus one. Read independently of
/// `reading_order` by counting predecessors, so the two must agree: `reading_rank(graph, order[k]) == k + 1`. Refuses `BadObjectLabel`
/// for a background or past-the-count label.
pub fn reading_rank(graph: scene_graph.SceneGraph, a: u32) !u32 {
    // invariant: the manifest holds exactly one row per component.
    assert(graph.manifest.objects.len == @as(usize, graph.components));
    if (a == 0 or a > graph.components) return error.BadObjectLabel;
    const ea = try scene_manifest.object_of(graph.manifest, a);

    var rank: u32 = 1;
    var b: u32 = 1;
    while (b <= graph.components) : (b += 1) {
        if (b == a) continue;
        const eb = try scene_manifest.object_of(graph.manifest, b);
        if (entry_reads_before(eb, ea)) rank += 1;
    }
    // invariant: a real object holds a real seat, between the first and the last.
    assert(rank >= 1);
    assert(rank <= graph.components);
    return rank;
}

// --- selftest: the DECISIVE partial-to-total crux -- an L and a block in its concavity (overlapping boxes, every strict flag FALSE, yet
//     the reading order seats the L first: the total order outruns the flags); the L strictly above the lower objects (a_above_b TRUE and
//     the reading order agrees: it refines, never contradicts); a flag-shaped object labeled AFTER a small block yet reading BEFORE it
//     (the order reorders the labels, no mere relabeling); a strict total order (antisymmetry, totality, transitivity); rank agrees with
//     the sequence; refusals by name; determinism ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Mint a one-color RGBA canvas -- a local, bounded builder so this rung leans on no higher module.
fn make_canvas(garden: std.mem.Allocator, w: u32, h: u32, fill: u8) !qoi.Pixmap {
    // invariant: a canvas has real extent on both axes.
    assert(w >= 1);
    assert(h >= 1);
    const total: u64 = @as(u64, w) * @as(u64, h);
    // invariant: the canvas stays within the image module's named pixel ceiling.
    assert(total <= qoi.qoi_max_pixels);
    const pixels = try garden.alloc(u8, @as(usize, @intCast(total)) * 4);
    var i: usize = 0;
    while (i < pixels.len) : (i += 4) {
        pixels[i] = fill;
        pixels[i + 1] = fill;
        pixels[i + 2] = fill;
        pixels[i + 3] = 255;
    }
    return qoi.Pixmap{ .width = w, .height = h, .channels = 4, .colorspace = 0, .pixels = pixels };
}

/// Set one foreground pixel at (x, y).
fn set_dot(img: *qoi.Pixmap, x: u32, y: u32) void {
    // invariant: the write stays inside the buffer on both axes.
    assert(x < img.width);
    assert(y < img.height);
    const di = (@as(usize, y) * @as(usize, img.width) + @as(usize, x)) * 4;
    img.pixels[di] = 255;
    img.pixels[di + 1] = 255;
    img.pixels[di + 2] = 255;
    img.pixels[di + 3] = 255;
}

fn run_selftest(garden: std.mem.Allocator) !u8 {
    // --- BUILD THE SCENE -- four objects on a 12x12 canvas, labeled in raster order (first-encountered pixel):
    //     L (label 1): column x=1 rows 1..5 + row y=5 cols 1..5, box (1,1)-(5,5);
    //     M (label 2): a 2x2 block cols 3..4 rows 2..3 tucked in L's concavity, box (3,2)-(4,3);
    //     Y (label 3): a 2x1 block at (4,7),(5,7), box (4,7)-(5,7), its first pixel (4,7);
    //     X (label 4): a flag -- a right-top nub at (8,7),(9,7),(8,8),(9,8) and a body along row 9 cols 3..9, box (3,7)-(9,9), first
    //     pixel (8,7). X's TOP ROW starts at x=8 (later than Y's first pixel x=4, so X labels AFTER Y) while its BODY reaches to x=3
    //     (leftward of Y's x=4, so X reads BEFORE Y) ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    var scene = try make_canvas(garden, 12, 12, 0);
    var ly: u32 = 1;
    while (ly <= 5) : (ly += 1) set_dot(&scene, 1, ly); // L vertical arm
    var lx: u32 = 1;
    while (lx <= 5) : (lx += 1) set_dot(&scene, lx, 5); // L horizontal arm
    // M -- the block in the concavity
    set_dot(&scene, 3, 2);
    set_dot(&scene, 4, 2);
    set_dot(&scene, 3, 3);
    set_dot(&scene, 4, 3);
    // Y -- the small 2x1 block
    set_dot(&scene, 4, 7);
    set_dot(&scene, 5, 7);
    // X -- the flag: right-top nub then a leftward body along row 9
    set_dot(&scene, 8, 7);
    set_dot(&scene, 9, 7);
    set_dot(&scene, 8, 8);
    set_dot(&scene, 9, 8);
    var bx: u32 = 3;
    while (bx <= 9) : (bx += 1) set_dot(&scene, bx, 9); // body row 9 cols 3..9

    const map = try label.label_components(garden, &scene);
    if (map.count != 4) return error.SceneNotFourComponents;
    const manifest = try scene_manifest.build_manifest(garden, &map);

    // confirm the raster labels and boxes are exactly the four intended
    const l_obj = try scene_manifest.object_of(manifest, 1);
    const m_obj = try scene_manifest.object_of(manifest, 2);
    const y_obj = try scene_manifest.object_of(manifest, 3);
    const x_obj = try scene_manifest.object_of(manifest, 4);
    if (l_obj.min_x != 1 or l_obj.min_y != 1 or l_obj.max_x != 5 or l_obj.max_y != 5) return error.LBoxWrong;
    if (m_obj.min_x != 3 or m_obj.min_y != 2 or m_obj.max_x != 4 or m_obj.max_y != 3) return error.MBoxWrong;
    if (y_obj.min_x != 4 or y_obj.min_y != 7 or y_obj.max_x != 5 or y_obj.max_y != 7) return error.YBoxWrong;
    if (x_obj.min_x != 3 or x_obj.min_y != 7 or x_obj.max_x != 9 or x_obj.max_y != 9) return error.XBoxWrong;

    const graph = try scene_graph.build_scene_graph(garden, manifest, &map);

    // --- THE READING ORDER -- sort by (top edge, left edge, label): L (1,1), M (2,3), X (7,3), Y (7,4) -- so the sequence is
    //     [1, 2, 4, 3]: X reads before Y though X is the LATER label -----------------------------------------------------------------
    const order = try reading_order(garden, graph);
    if (order.len != 4) return error.OrderNotFour;
    if (order[0] != 1) return error.FirstNotL;
    if (order[1] != 2) return error.SecondNotM;
    if (order[2] != 4) return error.ThirdNotX;
    if (order[3] != 3) return error.FourthNotY;

    // the order is a PERMUTATION -- every label once, so the four seats hold the four distinct objects
    var seen: [4]bool = .{ false, false, false, false };
    var oi: usize = 0;
    while (oi < order.len) : (oi += 1) {
        const lbl = order[oi];
        if (lbl < 1 or lbl > 4) return error.OrderLabelOutOfRange;
        if (seen[@as(usize, lbl) - 1]) return error.OrderNotPermutation;
        seen[@as(usize, lbl) - 1] = true;
    }

    // --- THE ORDER EARNS ITS KEEP -- X is the LATER raster label (4) than Y (3), yet X reads BEFORE Y: the reading order genuinely
    //     reorders the labels, no mere relabeling -- because X's body reaches to x=3, leftward of Y's x=4 --------------------------
    if (x_obj.label <= y_obj.label) return error.XNotLaterLabel; // 4 > 3
    if (!try reads_before(graph, 4, 3)) return error.XNotBeforeY; // yet X reads first
    if (try reads_before(graph, 3, 4)) return error.YFalselyBeforeX;
    if (x_obj.min_x >= y_obj.min_x) return error.XBodyNotLeftOfY; // 3 < 4, the reason

    // --- THE READING ORDER OUTRUNS THE STRICT FLAGS -- L and M have OVERLAPPING boxes (M in L's concavity), so every strict flag reads
    //     FALSE, the scene graph leaving the pair unordered; yet reads_before(L, M) is TRUE, the total order seating a first where the
    //     flags were silent. The reversal pair X and Y say it again: overlapping boxes, no flag, yet the order seats X first ---------
    const rel_lm = try scene_graph.relation_of(graph, 1, 2);
    if (!rel_lm.boxes_overlap) return error.LMBoxesNotOverlapping;
    if (rel_lm.a_left_of_b or rel_lm.a_right_of_b or rel_lm.a_above_b or rel_lm.a_below_b) return error.LMFalselyDirectional;
    if (!try reads_before(graph, 1, 2)) return error.LNotBeforeM; // total order decides the flag-silent pair
    const rel_xy = try scene_graph.relation_of(graph, 4, 3);
    if (!rel_xy.boxes_overlap) return error.XYBoxesNotOverlapping;
    if (rel_xy.a_left_of_b or rel_xy.a_right_of_b or rel_xy.a_above_b or rel_xy.a_below_b) return error.XYFalselyDirectional;

    // --- THE READING ORDER REFINES, IT NEVER CONTRADICTS -- where the boxes ARE strictly separated (L wholly above X, M wholly above Y,
    //     so a_above_b reads TRUE), the reading order AGREES, seating the upper object first ------------------------------------------
    const rel_lx = try scene_graph.relation_of(graph, 1, 4);
    if (!rel_lx.a_above_b) return error.LNotStrictlyAboveX;
    if (!try reads_before(graph, 1, 4)) return error.OrderContradictsFlagLX;
    const rel_my = try scene_graph.relation_of(graph, 2, 3);
    if (!rel_my.a_above_b) return error.MNotStrictlyAboveY;
    if (!try reads_before(graph, 2, 3)) return error.OrderContradictsFlagMY;

    // --- A STRICT TOTAL ORDER -- for every distinct pair exactly one of reads_before(a,b), reads_before(b,a) is true (antisymmetry and
    //     totality together); and it is transitive: L before M and M before X gives L before X --------------------------------------
    var pa: u32 = 1;
    while (pa <= 4) : (pa += 1) {
        var pb: u32 = 1;
        while (pb <= 4) : (pb += 1) {
            if (pa == pb) continue;
            const ab = try reads_before(graph, pa, pb);
            const ba = try reads_before(graph, pb, pa);
            if (ab == ba) return error.OrderNotTotalOrAntisymmetric; // exactly one holds
        }
    }
    if (!try reads_before(graph, 1, 2)) return error.TransSetupLM;
    if (!try reads_before(graph, 2, 4)) return error.TransSetupMX;
    if (!try reads_before(graph, 1, 4)) return error.OrderNotTransitive;

    // --- RANK AGREES WITH THE SEQUENCE -- reading_rank, read independently by counting predecessors, gives each object's 1-based seat,
    //     so it must equal its position in the order ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    var ri: usize = 0;
    while (ri < order.len) : (ri += 1) {
        const seat = try reading_rank(graph, order[ri]);
        if (seat != @as(u32, @intCast(ri)) + 1) return error.RankDisagreesWithOrder;
    }

    // --- REFUSALS -- a reads-before of an object with itself and a background or past-the-count label refuse BadObjectLabel; the rank of
    //     a bad label refuses BadObjectLabel, all having read nothing ------------------------------------------------------------------
    if (reads_before(graph, 2, 2)) |_| return error.SelfNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.BadObjectLabel) return error.WrongSelfError;
    }
    if (reads_before(graph, 1, 5)) |_| return error.PastNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.BadObjectLabel) return error.WrongPastError;
    }
    if (reads_before(graph, 0, 2)) |_| return error.ZeroNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.BadObjectLabel) return error.WrongZeroError;
    }
    if (reading_rank(graph, 5)) |_| return error.BadRankNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.BadObjectLabel) return error.WrongBadRankError;
    }

    // --- A SINGLE-OBJECT SCENE -- one block, so the order is [1], its rank 1, and reads_before refuses BadObjectLabel (no second object
    //     to compare) --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    var lone_img = try make_canvas(garden, 6, 6, 0);
    var qy: u32 = 1;
    while (qy <= 2) : (qy += 1) {
        var qx: u32 = 1;
        while (qx <= 2) : (qx += 1) set_dot(&lone_img, qx, qy);
    }
    const lone_map = try label.label_components(garden, &lone_img);
    if (lone_map.count != 1) return error.LoneNotOneComponent;
    const lone_manifest = try scene_manifest.build_manifest(garden, &lone_map);
    const lone_graph = try scene_graph.build_scene_graph(garden, lone_manifest, &lone_map);
    const lone_order = try reading_order(garden, lone_graph);
    if (lone_order.len != 1 or lone_order[0] != 1) return error.LoneOrderWrong;
    if (try reading_rank(lone_graph, 1) != 1) return error.LoneRankNotOne;
    if (reads_before(lone_graph, 1, 2)) |_| return error.LonePairNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.BadObjectLabel) return error.WrongLoneError;
    }

    // --- DETERMINISM -- the same scene reads the same order twice ----------------------------------------------------------------
    const o1 = try reading_order(garden, graph);
    const o2 = try reading_order(garden, graph);
    var di: usize = 0;
    while (di < o1.len) : (di += 1) {
        if (o1[di] != o2[di]) return error.OrderNotDeterministic;
    }

    print("GREEN image-reading-order: the whole scene's TOTAL READING ORDER -- fold scene_graph's per-pair directional flags into one top-to-bottom, left-to-right page order, exact integers, NO float and NO tolerance -- the DECISIVE box-is-a-prefilter crux lifted one last step, from a PARTIAL relation to a TOTAL order. THE READING ORDER OUTRUNS THE STRICT FLAGS -- an L (label 1, box (1,1)-(5,5)) and a small block tucked in its concavity (label 2, box (3,2)-(4,3)) have OVERLAPPING boxes, so all four of object_relation's strict directional flags read FALSE and the scene graph leaves the pair UNORDERED, yet the L's top edge sits above the block's so reads_before(L, block) is TRUE: the total order names a first where every strict flag is silent, and the same holds for the reversal pair. THE READING ORDER REFINES, IT NEVER CONTRADICTS -- where the boxes ARE strictly separated (the L wholly above a lower object, a_above_b TRUE), the reading order AGREES and seats the upper object first: it adds order where the flags were silent and reverses none they asserted. THE ORDER EARNS ITS KEEP -- a flag-shaped object whose top row starts at x=8 (labeled AFTER a small block at x=4) yet whose body reaches to x=3 (leftward of the block's x=4) reads BEFORE that block, so the sequence 1,2,4,3 genuinely reorders the raster labels 1,2,3,4, no mere relabeling. The order is a PERMUTATION and a STRICT TOTAL order -- for every distinct pair exactly one of reads_before holds (antisymmetry and totality), and it is transitive (L before M and M before X gives L before X); reading_rank read independently by counting predecessors agrees with each object's seat in the sequence; a reads-before of an object with itself and a background or past-the-count label refuse BadObjectLabel having read nothing, a single-object scene orders to one seat; the same scene reads the same order twice. Compose not braid over image/scene_graph.rye (the pairwise flag matrix the order refines), image/scene_manifest.rye (the per-object boxes the comparator reads), and image/label.rye (the component map)\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    const garden = init.arena.allocator();
    const args = try init.minimal.args.toSlice(garden);
    if (args.len >= 2 and std.mem.eql(u8, args[1], "selftest")) {
        return run_selftest(garden);
    }
    print("usage: reading_order selftest\n", .{});
    return 1;
}
